  translation: "1. Dhritarashtra said: Gathered on the field of dharma, the field of the Kurus, and eager to fight, what did my men and the sons of Pandu do, Sanjaya?\n2. Sanjaya said: Then, seeing the Pandava army drawn up, King Duryodhana approached the teacher and spoke these words.\n3. Look, teacher, at this great army of the sons of Pandu, drawn up by your wise pupil, the son of Drupada.\n4. Here are heroes, great archers, equal in battle to Bhima and Arjuna: Yuyudhana, Virata, and Drupada, a great chariot-warrior,\n5. Dhrishtaketu, Chekitana, and the valiant king of Kashi, Purujit, Kuntibhoja, and Shaibya, a bull among men,\n6. the valiant Yudhamanyu and the brave Uttamaujas, the son of Subhadra and the sons of Draupadi, all of them great chariot-warriors.\n7. Know also, best of the twice-born, those who are distinguished on our side. I name them to you, the leaders of my army, so that you may know them.\n8. Yourself and Bhishma, Karna and Kripa, victorious in battle, Ashvatthaman, Vikarna, and Jayadratha, the son of Somadatta,\n9. and many other heroes, ready to give up their lives for my sake, armed with many kinds of weapons, all skilled in war.\n10. That army of ours, guarded by Bhishma, is inadequate, while this army of theirs, guarded by Bhima, is adequate.\n11. So, stationed each in his own place in every division, all of you must guard Bhishma.\n12. To raise his spirits, the eldest of the Kurus, the grandsire, mighty in his glory, roared like a lion and blew his conch.\n13. Then conches and kettledrums, tabors, drums, and cow-horns burst out all at once, and that sound became a tumult.\n14. Then Madhava and the son of Pandu, standing in a great chariot yoked with white horses, blew their divine conches.\n15. Hrishikesha blew the Panchajanya, and Dhananjaya the Devadatta. Vrikodara, whose deeds are terrible, blew his great conch, the Paundra.\n16. King Yudhishthira, the son of Kunti, blew the Anantavijaya, and Nakula and Sahadeva blew the Sughosha and the Manipushpaka.\n17. The king of Kashi, a superb archer, and Shikhandin, a great chariot-warrior, Dhrishtadyumna and Virata, and Satyaki the unconquered,\n18. Drupada and the sons of Draupadi, all together, O lord of the earth, and the mighty-armed son of Subhadra, blew their conches, each his own.\n19. That roar tore the hearts of Dhritarashtra's men, and the tumult filled both sky and earth with its sound.\n20. Then, seeing Dhritarashtra's men drawn up, as the clash of weapons began, the son of Pandu, whose banner is a monkey, took up his bow\n21. Arjuna said: O lord of the earth, he then spoke this word to Hrishikesha: Place my chariot between the two armies, Acyuta.\n22. while I look over these men standing here eager to fight, and see with whom I must fight in this undertaking of battle.\n23. I will look at those gathered here who are about to fight, wishing to please the evil-minded son of Dhritarashtra in the war.\n24. Sanjaya said: O descendant of Bharata, Hrishikesha, so addressed by Gudakesha, drew up that finest of chariots between the two armies\n25. in front of Bhishma and Drona and all the kings, and said: Son of Pritha, behold these Kurus gathered here.\n26. There the son of Pritha saw, standing, fathers and grandfathers, teachers, maternal uncles, brothers, sons, grandsons, and companions,\n27. fathers-in-law and friends as well, in both armies. Looking on all those kinsmen standing there, the son of Kunti\n28. Arjuna said: Overcome by deep pity, he spoke this in despair: Seeing these kinsmen arrayed and eager to fight, Krishna,\n29. my limbs sink, and my mouth is dry. A trembling comes over my body, and my hair stands on end.\n30. The Gandiva slips from my hand, and my skin burns. I cannot stand, and my mind seems to reel.\n31. And I see omens of disaster, Keshava. I see no good in killing my own people in battle.\n32. I do not desire victory, Krishna, nor a kingdom, nor pleasures. What use is a kingdom to us, Govinda, or enjoyments, or life itself?\n33. Those for whose sake we desired a kingdom, enjoyments, and pleasures stand here in battle, having given up their lives and their wealth.\n34. Teachers, fathers, sons, and grandfathers as well, maternal uncles, fathers-in-law, grandsons, brothers-in-law, and kinsmen too.\n35. I do not want to kill them, Madhusudana, even if they kill me, even for the sake of kingship over the three worlds. How much less for the sake of the earth.\n36. What joy would be ours, Janardana, in killing Dhritarashtra's sons? Sin alone would come upon us if we killed these attackers.\n37. Therefore we should not kill Dhritarashtra's sons along with their kinsmen. How could we be happy, Madhava, after killing our own people?\n38. Even if these men, their minds overcome by greed, do not see the evil done by destroying a family, or the sin in betraying friends,\n39. why should we not know enough to turn back from this sin, we who see the evil done by destroying a family, Janardana?\n40. When a family is destroyed, its ancient duties perish. When dharma is lost, adharma overcomes the whole family.\n41. When adharma prevails, Krishna, the women of the family are corrupted. When the women are corrupted, Varshneya, the orders of society are mixed.\n42. This mixing leads the family, and those who destroyed it, to hell. Their forefathers fall, the offerings of rice and water having ceased.\n43. By these evils of the men who destroy a family, evils that mix the orders of society, the everlasting duties of caste and of family are uprooted.\n44. We have heard, Janardana, that for men whose family duties have been destroyed, a dwelling in hell is certain.\n45. Alas, we are resolved to commit a great sin, in that greed for the joys of a kingdom has set us to kill our own people.\n46. If the sons of Dhritarashtra, weapons in hand, should kill me in battle while I do not resist and hold no weapon, that would be better for me.\n47. Sanjaya said: Having spoken so, Arjuna sat down on the seat of the chariot in the midst of the battle, casting aside his bow and arrow, his mind stricken with grief.",
